From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 05:00:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Strip the staging out of a single-speaker shot instead of
 arguing with it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 16 appended "This shot frames only the speaking character" to the
scene's authored direction and left that direction intact. So every shot of
a two-hander asked for both of them and then for one of them:

  "Keep both faces ... centered. Bathroom mirror selfie setup. Ava holds a
   medication box ...; Ben appears beside her ... This shot frames only the
   speaking character."

Two contradictory instructions, and the model follows whichever half is
more concrete. Authored staging is always more concrete than a framing note
tacked on after it, so all nine dialogue shots of job #51875 came back with
two people in them, and the mirror scene handed the sync model four faces —
two actors and their reflections — and crashed it.

Appending cannot be made to work. The staging has to be removed.

`settingOnly` drops any clause that names someone other than this shot's
speaker, and any clause carrying a multi-subject cue — both, they, together,
beside, each other, and so on. Mirrors, reflections and selfies go with
them: a reflection is a second face even when it is the speaker's own, which
is the specific thing that killed scene1-s2. What survives is setting.

Stripping this hard is safe because prose is not what holds a guided scene's
location together. Every scene renders against a frozen approved backdrop
image and the prompt already orders the model to preserve it. The words only
have to avoid contradicting the picture — so a direction reduced to almost
nothing still renders the right room.

The rebuilt direction leads with the constraint and trails the setting,
which is the same lesson as scoping the character reference: state the rule
before the material it governs, and make the negatives rules rather than
preferences — "no second face, no mirror, no reflection" rather than a
preference for one person.

The turn is now explicitly slight and front-facing. A full profile is the
one thing every lip-sync model fails on, so the eyeline has to sell the
conversation without turning the mouth away from camera.

